        "summary":  "Temporal policies in Amazon Bedrock AgentCore enable stateful, trajectory-aware authorization rules that evaluate agent requests against full session history at the gateway perimeter. This approach prevents security failures like data fabrication, workflow violations, and unauthorized cumulative exposure that stateless per-request policies cannot detect. By operating outside the agent\u0027s own code, the controls provide tamper-proof enforcement that is independent of model behavior.",
        "execSummary":  "Traditional security models that evaluate each API call in isolation are insufficient for AI agents that operate over long, multi-step sessions ΓÇö this post introduces a pattern where security rules are evaluated against the full action history, which is the correct architecture for agentic systems in enterprise environments. For security and compliance leaders, the key insight is that agent security requires a new class of controls that understand workflow context, not just individual requests ΓÇö this has direct implications for how your security team should review and approve agentic AI deployments. The tamper-proof, perimeter-based enforcement model is important because it removes the ability for a compromised or hallucinating agent to bypass its own guardrails. Enterprises evaluating agentic platforms should ask vendors specifically whether security enforcement is model-external or model-internal.",
